@page "/learn/displayname-component"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>DisplayName · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">DisplayName</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">Your form's model already names every field with <code>[Display]</code>. Why does the grid type them again?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Forms · Any render mode · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a component called <code>DisplayName</code>, used as
        <code>&lt;DisplayName For="..." /&gt;</code>, that renders a property's <code>[Display]</code> name as plain
        text. In .NET 10 validation messages already read <code>[Display]</code>, but nothing in Blazor rendered it as
        text, the job MVC's <code>@@Html.DisplayNameFor</code> helper did.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>Suppliers</i> grid. Its columns are the fields of the <i>New supplier</i> form, and that form's
        model names each one with <code>[Display]</code>, in English and in German.
    </p>
    <p>
        The grid's headers are written the .NET 10 way: two have a typed <code>Title</code>, and one has none. Above
        the grid, a dashed <b>Guide readout</b> lists the model's names as they are now. It isn't part of the technique;
        it's there to compare against.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The grid, with typed headers" Url="demo/displayname/suppliers/net10"
                Label=".NET 10: headers typed as Title" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            Before you press anything, look at the third header: <b>CONTACT</b>, the property's own name, while the
            readout says Main contact. Then click <b>Deutsch</b> at the top of the frame. The readout turns German
            (Lieferantencode, Firmenname, Hauptansprechpartner), but every header stays English.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>A typed header is a copy, and copies don't follow</h2>
    <p>The name lives in one place, but the grid doesn't read it:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Where each header comes from</span>
        <pre class="snip">SupplierForm.Code     <span class="dim">[Display] Supplier code / Lieferantencode</span>
  └─ header: Title="Supplier code"     <span class="err">typed again, English only</span>
SupplierForm.Company  <span class="dim">[Display] Company name / Firmenname</span>
  └─ header: Title="Company name"      <span class="err">typed again, English only</span>
SupplierForm.Contact  <span class="dim">[Display] Main contact / Hauptansprechpartner</span>
  └─ header: no Title                  <span class="err">"Contact", the property name</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <code>[Display]</code> looks its name up in the request's culture, so the readout follows the language. A
        typed <code>Title</code> is a fixed string. Rename or translate a field on the model and the grid keeps the
        old words. Leave <code>Title</code> out and QuickGrid prints the raw property name.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Point each header at the form's model</h2>
    <p>Put a <code>&lt;DisplayName For&gt;</code> in each column's <code>HeaderTemplate</code>.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole fix, for one column</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;PropertyColumn Property="s =&gt; s.Contact"&gt;
    &lt;HeaderTemplate&gt;<span class="hl">&lt;DisplayName For="() =&gt; form.Contact" /&gt;</span>&lt;/HeaderTemplate&gt;
&lt;/PropertyColumn&gt;

<span class="hl">SupplierForm form = null!;</span>   <span class="dim">// never dereferenced</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        <b>Why a <code>HeaderTemplate</code>, not <code>Title</code>:</b> <code>DisplayName</code> is a component, and
        .NET 11 adds no method that returns the name as a string, so there's nothing to put in <code>Title</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>Why point at the form's model:</b> the <code>[Display]</code> names live on <code>SupplierForm</code>. The
        grid's row type, the <code>Supplier</code> entity, has none, so pointing <code>For</code> at a
        <code>Supplier</code> would print "Contact" again. And inside <code>HeaderTemplate</code> there is no row: its
        <code>context</code> is the column. So the page keeps a <code>SupplierForm</code> field just to name
        properties on. <code>For</code> is read for the property it names and never run, so the field can stay
        <code>null</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
        <code>DisplayName</code> reads <code>[Display]</code> each time it renders, through
        <code>DisplayAttribute.GetName()</code>, so it gets the request's language.
    </p>
    <p>
        <b>The cost: a <code>HeaderTemplate</code> replaces QuickGrid's sort button.</b> On a sortable column, put
        the button back inside the template yourself. Falling back to a typed <code>Title</code> would bring the
        original problem back. (Sorting needs an interactive render mode either way; the demos below are static and
        don't sort.)
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">A sortable column with DisplayName</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;PropertyColumn Property="s =&gt; s.Contact" Sortable="true"&gt;
    &lt;HeaderTemplate&gt;
        <span class="hl">&lt;button @@onclick="() =&gt; context.Grid.SortByColumnAsync(context)"&gt;</span>
            &lt;DisplayName For="() =&gt; form.Contact" /&gt;
        <span class="hl">&lt;/button&gt;</span>
    &lt;/HeaderTemplate&gt;
&lt;/PropertyColumn&gt;</pre>
    </div>
    <p>Here's the same grid, with the fix on every column.</p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same grid, with DisplayName" Url="demo/displayname/suppliers"
                Label=".NET 11: DisplayName">
        <Caption>
            The third header now reads <b>Main contact</b>, matching the readout. Click <b>Deutsch</b> at the top of
            the frame: the headers
            turn German with the readout. The German comes from the model's resource file; none is typed in the grid.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the page behind Fig. 2, the model it points at, and the page behind Fig. 1:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/DisplayNames/Suppliers.razor" Feature="displayname-component">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 2's grid. Each header points at <code>SupplierForm</code>, the form's model, not at the grid's row
            type. <code>&lt;FormLabels /&gt;</code> is the Guide readout above the grid, not part of the technique.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/DisplayNames/SupplierForm.cs" Feature="displayname-component">
        <Caption>
            Not new: the model where each name is set once. <code>ResourceType</code> makes each name a key, looked
            up in the request's culture. Both figures' readouts read it too.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="5" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/DisplayNames/SuppliersNet10.razor" Feature="displayname-component">
        <Caption>
            Fig. 1's grid: two names typed again as <code>Title</code>, and one missing.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>A second copy of the name, or a helper</h2>
    <p>Fig. 1 shows the plain typed <code>Title</code>. The other ways a .NET 10 grid could name a column:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Column headers in .NET 10</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;PropertyColumn Property="s =&gt; s.Contact"
    Title="@@L["MainContact"]" /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why"><code>IStringLocalizer</code> looks the key up per request</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ translates, but it's a second key beside the model's: rename the field on the model and the header keeps the old name</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">Title="@@DisplayNames.For(() =&gt; form.Contact)"</pre>
                <span class="why">a reflection helper you write, reading <code>DisplayAttribute</code> from the expression</span>
                <div class="verdict ok">✓ works, and keeps sorting; one more helper to write, test and copy between projects</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;DisplayName For="..." /&gt;</pre>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ warning RZ10012, "unexpected name 'DisplayName'": an empty, unknown element, so the headers are blank</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>A component that renders the name</h2>
    <p>
        <code>&lt;DisplayName For&gt;</code> is the text-only sibling of <code>&lt;Label For&gt;</code>, Blazor's
        version of MVC's <code>DisplayNameFor</code>. It renders just the name, with no wrapping element, so it fits
        in a header, a table cell or a sentence. That's what Fig. 2 runs.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>It reads attributes, not <code>IStringLocalizer</code>.</b> If your app translates through <code>IStringLocalizer</code> keys, as in the <code>Title="@@L[...]"</code> option above, <code>DisplayName</code> won't see them: move the names to <code>[Display(ResourceType = ...)]</code>, as this model does.</li>
        <li><b>Any render mode.</b> These demos are static SSR; <code>DisplayName</code> renders the same text when interactive.</li>
        <li><b>Falls back in order.</b> <code>[Display(Name)]</code>, then <code>[DisplayName]</code>, then the property's own name.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
